  "text": "Our observation of a threshold effect contrasts with that of a study in a Japanese cohort, which found no evidence of a threshold in the ABSI-diabetes association [30]. In that cohort, ABSI was associated with a consistent, graded increase in Type 2 diabetes risk across the full range of values, with no apparent non-linearity even after multivariable adjustment [30]. Several factors may explain this discrepancy, most plausibly differences in the racial and regional composition of the two study populations. Our analysis drew on NHANES and therefore reflects the U.S. population, whereas the comparison study was conducted in a Japanese cohort [30]. Such compositional differences may plausibly explain the divergence in the shape of the ABSI-diabetes association [44–49]. Asian and non-Asian populations differ in body-fat distribution, insulin sensitivity, and diabetes-related genetic variants, and these characteristics may in turn modify the sensitivity and specificity of ABSI for diabetes risk [44–46]. Lifestyle and environmental factors (including dietary habits, physical activity, and socioeconomic status) also vary across regions and may further contribute to differences in diabetes risk [47–49]."
}

